well some residents facing evictions say  the new moratorium announced this week  really just isn't enough  a local organization helping immigrant  workers held a rally today  joe moeller shows us what organizers say  needs to happen  people who were already evicted and  others who are now facing eviction say  they want the governor's help people  came here  outside the grant sawyer building spaced  out to send a message  arriba the las vegas workers center  organized the rally they say the  governor's moratorium implemented this  week does not do enough  they say they want a number of things  changed including a closure of eviction  courts and a moratorium on utility  shutoffs during the holidays  many of the people here were immigrant  workers organizers say not enough is  being done to protect them  when this pandemic starts i lost my job  and after that i lost my apartment and  asking  him to help families like me because i  am single mom  of three kids and they are suffering  a lot so i'm asking him to help us we  spoke to many people who were evicted  and many who are worried about  electricity being cut very soon here  people say immigrant workers need more  protection financially and from the  virus  the organizer we spoke with from ariba  is worried that if something is not done  soon here more people will end up  homeless reporting in las vegas joe  moeller 8 news now
 

